opportunity for for basically for the

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:22.710
synthesis tools to make best use of the

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:23.990
resources

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.990
The recommendation is to use active high

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.750
controls and and certainly you should

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:30.550
always be consistent in your designs but

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.310
uh active high if you don't do that it

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.750
may have to invert them. It has to add

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.630
in extra logic potentially avoid having

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:38.389
set and reset on the same flip-flop. As

00:11:38.399 --> 00:11:40.949
I said you actually you can't what what

00:11:40.959 --> 00:11:42.550
it will do is it will try and synthesize

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:44.389
what you've asked it to when we come to

00:11:44.399 --> 00:11:45.910
actually look at the designs and it will

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.230
end up with something quite complicated.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.069
So when you're doing your design using

00:11:50.079 --> 00:11:52.630
VHDL, you need to kind of be thinking

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:54.710
about a little bit about these things so

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:55.910
you don't give a design that the

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.150
synthesis tools will end up producing

00:11:58.160 --> 00:11:59.843
something that takes a lot of uh

00:11:59.853 --> 00:12:02.230
a lot of the blocks or does

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:03.430
something quite complicated with

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.269
horrible delays. You have to be aware of

00:12:05.279 --> 00:12:07.829
this when you're doing the design. Uh

00:12:07.839 --> 00:12:09.829
finally, avoid control signals and small

00:12:09.839 --> 00:12:12.230
shift registers and storage. And the

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:13.670
reason for that is if you don't have

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.150
control signals, it can use the lookup

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:19.670
tables instead of the flip-flops. Um and

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.509
and that actually and it therefore can

00:12:21.519 --> 00:12:23.670
implement um so if you think about it,

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:26.069
we've got these 64-bit

00:12:26.079 --> 00:12:28.710
um essentially 64-bit RAM if you want as

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:30.710
a lookup table that can be configured in

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.269
different ways. And if uh so that's a

00:12:33.279 --> 00:12:36.150
64-bit storage. If you have to use

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:37.509
flip-flops, you've only got eight of

00:12:37.519 --> 00:12:41.829
those. So 4 * 64 bits is obviously a lot

00:12:41.839 --> 00:12:44.389
more than um eight flip-flops on the

00:12:44.399 --> 00:12:46.470
same same block. So these are the kind

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.949
of basic principles we need to be using.

00:12:48.959 --> 00:12:50.790
And that's it in terms of the FPGA

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.030
architecture.

